    "result": "I implemented `RateLimiter` in `solution.py` and wrote a 41-case pytest suite in `tests/test_solution.py`; `python -m pytest -q tests` passes all 41. Each client keeps a time-ordered deque of `(time, cost)` records, and entries are dropped once `now - t >= window_seconds`, so a request exactly one window old no longer counts. `allow` only records accepted requests. `remaining` is `limit` minus units in use. `retry_after` walks the live records oldest first and returns the wait until enough units expire for the requested cost. Argument checks reject bools and non-integers for `limit` and `cost`, and a `now` smaller than the largest seen so far raises `ValueError` on every method. Two choices the spec left open: argument validation runs before the time check, and a call that raises does not advance the limiter's latest-seen time. The tests cover the spec rules, including multi-expiry `retry_after` and the no-state-change guarantees for rejected and read-only calls.",
